---
import ToolPageLayout from '../../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro';
import { UnixTimestampConverter as Converter } from '../../components/UnixTimestampConverter';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/content/ComparisonTable.astro';
import BestPractices from '../../components/content/BestPractices.astro';
import CommonMistakes from '../../components/content/CommonMistakes.astro';
import LanguageSupport from '../../components/content/LanguageSupport.astro';
import PrivacyBadge from '../../components/content/PrivacyBadge.astro';
import OfficialReferences from '../../components/content/OfficialReferences.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/content/RelatedTools.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is a Unix timestamp and how does it work?",
    answer: "A Unix timestamp (also known as Epoch time or POSIX time) is an integer value representing the total number of seconds that have elapsed since the Unix Epoch: January 1, 1970, at 00:00:00 Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), minus leap seconds. It is widely used in operating systems, file formats, and database systems to store dates and times as simple numeric variables, making temporal math and sorting extremely efficient."
  },
  {
    question: "Why is Unix time measured starting from January 1, 1970?",
    answer: "The date of January 1, 1970, was arbitrarily chosen as the epoch (the starting reference point) for the Unix operating system. The creators of Unix (Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie) needed a simple baseline to start counting system ticks. The initial systems measured time in fractions of a second, which was later standardized to standard seconds starting from Jan 1, 1970, for simplicity and cross-compatibility."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the Year 2038 problem (Y2038 bug)?",
    answer: "The Year 2038 problem occurs because many legacy operating systems and files represent Unix time as a 32-bit signed integer. The maximum value that can be represented by a 32-bit signed integer is <code>2,147,483,647</code>. This threshold will be reached on <strong>January 19, 2038, at 03:14:07 UTC</strong>. One second later, the integer will overflow and wrap around to a negative value (<code>-2,147,483,648</code>), which translates to December 13, 1901. Modern operating systems prevent this by upgrading timestamp variables to 64-bit integers, pushing the overflow limit billions of years into the future."
  },
  {
    question: "How do timezones affect a Unix timestamp?",
    answer: "Unix timestamps are entirely timezone-independent. By definition, a Unix timestamp represents the exact number of seconds since the epoch in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) everywhere in the world. When you convert a timestamp to a local date string, the local timezone offset (e.g., Eastern Standard Time or Indian Standard Time) is applied dynamically by the displaying device. The underlying timestamp value remains identical."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between Unix time in seconds vs milliseconds?",
    answer: "Standard Unix timestamps are represented in seconds (10 digits, e.g., <code>1718365200</code>). However, high-precision environments—including JavaScript, Java, and modern API protocols—utilize milliseconds (13 digits, e.g., <code>1718365200000</code>) to store fractional time. Microseconds (16 digits) and nanoseconds (19 digits) are standard in database kernels (like PostgreSQL) and language environments (like Go or Rust) to resolve micro-operations."
  },
  {
    question: "Does Unix time include leap seconds?",
    answer: "No, Unix time does not account for leap seconds. Unix time counts days as exactly 86,400 seconds each. When a leap second is officially added to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) to compensate for variations in Earth's rotation, the Unix clock repeats the last second of the day or adjusts backward, causing a minor deviation that systems resolve dynamically."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I get the current Unix timestamp in JavaScript or Node.js?",
    answer: "To retrieve the current timestamp in milliseconds, call <code>Date.now()</code>. To convert it into standard seconds, divide the value by 1,000 and round down: <code>Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)</code>. You can also construct a timestamp from a specific date string using <code>Date.parse('2026-06-14') / 1000</code>."
  },
  {
    question: "How can I parse natural relative dates (like '5 days ago') in programming?",
    answer: "In JavaScript, you can calculate relative dates by instantiating a new Date object, retrieving its timestamp, and subtracting or adding the offset in milliseconds. For example, to get '5 days ago', subtract the product of days, hours, minutes, seconds, and milliseconds: <code>new Date(Date.now() - 5 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000)</code>."
  },
  {
    question: "Is my pasted data safe and private when using this online converter?",
    answer: "Yes, 100% secure. Unlike traditional epoch converters that make API calls to backend servers to compute date formatting, useUtils executes all conversions, log parsing, and offset calculations inside your local browser sandbox. No parameters, strings, or logs are ever transmitted over the internet."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I convert a human date string back into a Unix epoch timestamp?",
    answer: "Most modern languages feature built-in date parsers. In JavaScript, you can pass any ISO 8601 or RFC 2822 date string into <code>new Date('date_string')</code> and call <code>.getTime()</code> to receive its millisecond epoch representation. In Python, use <code>datetime.fromisoformat('date_string').timestamp()</code>."
  },
  {
    question: "What is a Julian Date and how does it relate to Unix time?",
    answer: "A Julian Date is a continuous count of days since the beginning of the Julian Period (January 1, 4713 BC). While Unix time counts seconds from 1970, Julian Dates count fractional days from a historical astronomical epoch. Julian Dates are primarily used in astronomy to calculate differences between events separated by centuries. Our tool provides real-time translations between Unix epoch and Julian Dates."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does my converted timestamp result in a date in 1970?",
    answer: "This is a common digits mismatch error. If you pass a millisecond-level timestamp (13 digits) into a system expecting seconds (10 digits), the parser truncates or misinterprets the integer. For example, <code>1718365200000</code> parsed as seconds is interpreted as a date in the far future, while <code>1718365200</code> parsed as milliseconds returns <code>January 20, 1970</code> because it represents only 20 days since the epoch."
  },
  {
    question: "Can Unix timestamps be negative?",
    answer: "Yes, negative integers represent dates prior to the Unix Epoch. For example, a timestamp of <code>-315360000</code> represents January 1, 1960 (exactly 10 years before the Jan 1, 1970 baseline)."
  },
  {
    question: "How do database systems index Unix timestamps?",
    answer: "Because timestamps are standard integers, databases index them extremely quickly using standard B-Tree indexing. Integer comparisons are much faster than string comparisons (like standard date strings), which makes query filters like <code>WHERE created_at > 1718365200</code> highly performant."
  },
  {
    question: "What timezone is a Unix timestamp in?",
    answer: "By definition, Unix timestamps are always in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). There is no such thing as a timezone-specific Unix timestamp."
  },
  {
    question: "How does GPS time differ from Unix time?",
    answer: "GPS time counts seconds since its own epoch (January 6, 1980). Unlike Unix time, GPS time is a continuous timescale that is not adjusted for leap seconds. As a result, GPS time is ahead of UTC/Unix time by a number of seconds (currently 18 seconds)."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I format Unix time to ISO 8601 in Go?",
    answer: "In Go, you can format a timestamp by converting it to a <code>time.Time</code> struct and layout. Use <code>time.Unix(sec, 0).Format(time.RFC3339)</code> to yield a valid ISO 8601 format string."
  },
  {
    question: "Does the Unix Epoch account for leap years?",
    answer: "Yes. The conversion algorithms that translate seconds to days, months, and years account for leap years (including the leap year rules for centuries) so that the correct calendar representation is always returned."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I write timestamps in SQL Server?",
    answer: "In SQL Server, the <code>DATETIME2</code> or <code>DATETIMEOFFSET</code> types are preferred for representing dates. You can convert Unix timestamps using <code>DATEADD(s, unix_timestamp, '1970-01-01 00:00:00')</code>."
  },
  {
    question: "What is NTP and why is it important for timestamps?",
    answer: "The Network Time Protocol (NTP) synchronizes computer system clocks over packet-switched data networks. Without NTP, servers drift over time, which causes them to record slightly different Unix timestamps for the same event, causing logs out of order."
  },
  {
    question: "What is ISO 8601?",
    answer: "ISO 8601 is an international standard covering the exchange of date- and time-related data. It represents dates in a clear, unambiguous structure (e.g., <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss.sssZ</code>)."
  }
];

const headers = ["Resolution Level", "Digits", "Example Value", "Primary Ecosystems", "Storage Size"];
const rows = [
  {
    label: "Seconds (s)",
    values: ["10 digits", "<code>1718365200</code>", "Unix, Bash, Python, PHP, MySQL", "4 bytes (32-bit) / 8 bytes (64-bit)"]
  },
  {
    label: "Milliseconds (ms)",
    values: ["13 digits", "<code>1718365200000</code>", "JavaScript (Node.js), Java, MongoDB", "8 bytes"]
  },
  {
    label: "Microseconds (us)",
    values: ["16 digits", "<code>1718365200000000</code>", "PostgreSQL, Python Datetime micro-ops", "8 bytes"]
  },
  {
    label: "Nanoseconds (ns)",
    values: ["19 digits", "<code>1718365200000000000</code>", "Go, Rust, Linux Kernel timers", "8 bytes"]
  }
];

const practices = [
  {
    title: "Always Store Timestamps in 64-Bit Integers",
    description: "Future-proof your software against the Y2038 bug by declaring date columns as BIGINT (64-bit) in databases and int64 in application schemas."
  },
  {
    title: "Keep the Storage Layer in UTC",
    description: "Always store timestamps in UTC/Unix time in your database. Apply local timezone translations only in the final UI presentation layer."
  },
  {
    title: "Use ISO 8601 for API Exchange payloads",
    description: "When passing dates over REST or GraphQL APIs, use ISO 8601 strings (e.g. 2026-06-14T12:00:00Z) to ensure cross-platform compatibility."
  },
  {
    title: "Check Mismatched Digit Counts",
    description: "Always verify whether an incoming timestamp is in seconds (10 digits) or milliseconds (13 digits) to prevent dates reverting to 1970."
  }
];

const mistakes = [
  {
    title: "Dividing by 1000 in Floating Point Math",
    description: "In JavaScript, dividing milliseconds by 1000 without rounding leaves floating points (e.g. 1718365200.123), which can break database column insertion constraints.",
    fix: "Always use <code>Math.floor()</code> or <code>Math.round()</code> to convert milliseconds to integers."
  },
  {
    title: "Hardcoding Local Timezone Offsets",
    description: "Hardcoding timezone conversions (e.g. subtracting 5 hours manually for EST) breaks when daylight saving time (DST) shifts occur.",
    fix: "Leverage standard timezone libraries or native IANA timezone databases (like Intl.DateTimeFormat) to adjust offsets."
  },
  {
    title: "Assuming System Clock Accuracy",
    description: "Relying on local system clocks for server event ordering without NTP synchronization leads to clock drift and out-of-order databases.",
    fix: "Configure NTP synchronization on all distributed cloud containers and servers."
  }
];

const languages = [
  {
    lang: "JavaScript / Node.js",
    code: `
// Current seconds epoch
const sec = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
console.log('Seconds:', sec);

// Date to Epoch
const epoch = Math.floor(new Date('2026-06-14T12:00:00Z').getTime() / 1000);
console.log('Parsed Epoch:', epoch);

// Epoch to Date
const date = new Date(sec * 1000);
console.log('Local Date:', date.toString());
    `,
    note: "JS Date constructor expects milliseconds, so multiply Unix seconds by 1000."
  },
  {
    lang: "Python 3",
    code: `
import time
from datetime import datetime

# Current seconds epoch
sec = int(time.time())
print('Seconds:', sec)

# Date to Epoch
dt = datetime.fromisoformat('2026-06-14T12:00:00+00:00')
epoch = int(dt.timestamp())
print('Parsed Epoch:', epoch)

# Epoch to Date
local_date = datetime.fromtimestamp(sec)
print('Date object:', local_date)
    `,
    note: "Python's time.time() returns a float representing seconds. Cast to int for standard epochs."
  },
  {
    lang: "Go",
    code: `
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	// Current seconds epoch
	sec := time.Now().Unix()
	fmt.Println("Seconds:", sec)

	// Epoch to Date
	date := time.Unix(sec, 0)
	fmt.Println("Date:", date.Format(time.RFC3339))
}
    `,
    note: "Go provides high-precision timers, supporting Unix (seconds) and UnixNano (nanoseconds)."
  }
];

const references = [
  { title: "POSIX.1-2008 General Concepts: Seconds Since the Epoch", url: "https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/xbd/chap04.html#tag_04_15" },
  { title: "The Year 2038 Bug Information Center", url: "https://www.unix2038.com/" },
  { title: "MDN Web Docs: Intl.DateTimeFormat Timezone Parsing", url: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/DateTimeFormat" }
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Unix Timestamp Converter: Convert Epoch Time & Log Dates Online"
  description="Convert Unix epoch timestamps to human-readable dates and parse relative date strings locally. Features real-time ticking clocks, time scrubber sliders, offsets, Year 2038 warnings, and a batch log scanner."
  keywords="unix timestamp converter, epoch converter, epoch time converter, unix epoch converter, convert epoch date, log timestamp extractor, time scrubber, relative date parser, time math offsets, year 2038 bug"
  toolTitle="Unix Timestamp & Epoch Date Converter"
  toolDescription="Convert between Unix timestamps and standard dates in real-time. Scrub through timeline sliders, adjust temporal offsets, diagnose Year 2038 overflows, and extract batch epoch instances from server logs."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Converter client:load />

  <div slot="seo-content" class="flex flex-col gap-12 mt-10 max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full">
    <PrivacyBadge />

    <ComparisonTable 
      headers={headers} 
      rows={rows} 
      title="Epoch Metric Resolutions" 
      description="Compare representation lengths, ecosystems, and memory profiles across timestamp subdivisions:"
    />

    <BestPractices 
      practices={practices} 
      title="Epoch & Timestamp Best Practices" 
      description="Implement these guidelines to ensure index efficiency and bug-free date conversions in your schemas:"
    />

    <CommonMistakes 
      mistakes={mistakes} 
      title="Common Timestamp Pitfalls" 
      description="Avoid these common errors when writing temporal calculations and API database seed migrations:"
    />

    <LanguageSupport 
      languages={languages} 
      title="Programmatic Epoch Conversion" 
      description="Translate timestamps and convert epochs programmatically inside your local application stacks:"
    />

    <OfficialReferences 
      references={references} 
      title="Time Standards & Documentation" 
    />

    <RelatedTools />
  </div>
</ToolPageLayout>
